  "full_title": "The Head of John the Baptist brought to Herod: Predella Panel",
  "short_title": "The Head of John the Baptist brought to Herod",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "Gothic",
  "material": "egg tempera on wood",
  "dimensions": "31 × 37 cm",
  "production_date": "1454",
  "brief_description": "John the Baptist's head is presented to King Herod on a golden platter. He recoils in disgust, while his guests cover their eyes, unable to look. The girl in a pink dress is Salome, whose dancing had delighted Herod so much that he offered her whatever she wished for. Her mother, Herod's wife, asked her to demand the head of John the Baptist; she disliked John for criticising her marriage to Herod.\n\nThe arches in the background and the gestures and arrangement of the figures strongly resemble the sculpted bronze panel showing the same event which was made by Donatello and Giovanni di Turino for the font of the Baptistery in Siena in around 1427.",
